\title{Divisibility Bridges: Fibonacci Lattices, a Sharp Divisibility
Pigeonhole, and a Finite Garden-of-Eden Principle}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-18}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a self-contained development of three structurally linked, formally
verified results. First, we record the classical Fibonacci divisibility law
$m \mid n \Rightarrow F_m \mid F_n$ and upgrade it, for indices $m \ge 3$, to the
equivalence $F_m \mid F_n \iff m \mid n$, exhibiting the Fibonacci sequence as a
faithful embedding of the divisibility order of $\N$ into itself. Second, using
the $2$-adic odd-part decomposition of integers as a pigeonhole coloring, we
prove the sharp combinatorial theorem that any $(n+1)$-element subset of
$\{1,\dots,2n\}$ contains a divisibility pair. Third, we develop a finite
Garden-of-Eden theory: a state of a self-map $F$ is a Garden of Eden when it has
no preimage, and we prove (i) Gardens of Eden exist iff $F$ is not surjective,
(ii) iterates of an order-descending map form a descending chain, (iii) every
orbit of a monotone descending map on a finite poset of cardinality $N$
stabilizes within $N$ steps, and (iv) the finite Moore--Myhill shadow whereby
surjectivity forces injectivity on finite state spaces. The unifying theme is
that structure-preserving maps are governed by the structure they preserve.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A recurring motif across number theory, combinatorics, and dynamics is that a map
which respects an algebraic or order structure has its global behaviour forced by
that structure, often without explicit computation. This paper collects three
concrete incarnations and makes the bridges between them explicit.

The connecting thread is the interaction between a map and an invariant:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item the Fibonacci map $n \mapsto F_n$ respects divisibility, so its
        arithmetic copies that of indices;
  \item the odd-part map $x \mapsto \oddpart(x)$ records the $2$-adic valuation
        $v_2$ and, used as a pigeonhole color, yields a sharp extremal bound;
  \item an order-descending self-map on a finite poset respects $\le$, so
        finiteness forces stabilization and the failure of surjectivity forces
        permanently unreachable states.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Notation.} $\N$ denotes the non-negative integers; $F_n$ the $n$-th
Fibonacci number with $F_1=F_2=1$ (so $F_0=0$, $F_3=2$, $F_4=3$, $F_5=5$). For
$F\colon \alpha\to\alpha$, $F^{[n]}$ is the $n$-fold iterate with
$F^{[0]}=\mathrm{id}$. For $x\in\N$, $v_2(x)$ is the exponent of $2$ in $x$. We
write $[a,b]$ for the integer interval $\{a,\dots,b\}$.

\section{Fibonacci divisibility lattice}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{fib\_dvd\_of\_dvd}]\label{thm:fibdvd}
If $m \mid n$ then $F_m \mid F_n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
The divisibility direction of the classical theory. From the addition formula
$F_{a+b}=F_{a+1}F_b+F_a F_{b-1}$ one shows $F_a \mid F_{ka}$ by induction on $k$:
each increment by $a$ adds a multiple of $F_a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{fib\_dvd\_iff}]\label{thm:fibiff}
For $m \ge 3$,
\[
  F_m \mid F_n \iff m \mid n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
The reverse implication is Theorem~\ref{thm:fibdvd}. For the forward direction,
the engine is the identity $\gcd(F_m,F_n)=F_{\gcd(m,n)}$. If $F_m\mid F_n$ then
$\gcd(F_m,F_n)=F_m$, so $F_{\gcd(m,n)}=F_m$. Argue by contradiction: if
$m\nmid n$ then $g:=\gcd(m,n)$ is a proper divisor of $m$, hence $g<m$. Since
$m\ge 3$, strict monotonicity of $F$ on indices $\ge 2$ (with a finite check for
$g\in\{0,1\}$, where $F_g\le 1<2\le F_m$) gives $F_g<F_m$, contradicting
$F_g=F_m$. The bound $m\ge 3$ is sharp: $F_1=F_2=1$ divide every $F_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:fibiff} exhibits $n\mapsto F_n$ as an order embedding of
$(\N_{\ge 3},\mid)$ into $(\N,\mid)$: it both preserves and reflects divisibility.
\end{remark}

\section{A sharp divisibility pigeonhole}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{oddPart}]\label{def:odd}
For $x\in\N$,
\[
  \oddpart(x) := \frac{x}{2^{v_2(x)}},
\]
the odd part of $x$. For $x\ge 1$, $x=\oddpart(x)\cdot 2^{v_2(x)}$ uniquely with
$\oddpart(x)$ odd.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{divisibility\_pigeonhole}]\label{thm:pigeon}
Let $n\ge 1$ and let $S\subseteq[1,2n]$ with $|S|=n+1$. Then there exist
$a,b\in S$ with $a\ne b$ and $a\mid b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
Color $x\in S$ by $\oddpart(x)$. For $1\le x\le 2n$, $\oddpart(x)$ is odd and lies
in $[1,2n]$, so the colors land among $\{1,3,5,\dots,2n-1\}$, a set of exactly
$n$ values. Hence $|\oddpart(S)|\le n<n+1=|S|$, so by the pigeonhole principle
$x\mapsto\oddpart(x)$ is not injective on $S$: there are $a\ne b$ in $S$ with
$\oddpart(a)=\oddpart(b)=:q$. Write $a=q\,2^{i}$, $b=q\,2^{j}$; without loss of
generality $i\le j$, whence $b=a\cdot 2^{\,j-i}$ and $a\mid b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The bound $n+1$ is sharp: $\{n+1,\dots,2n\}$ has $n$ elements and no divisibility
pair, since distinct $a<b$ there satisfy $b<2a$. The hypothesis $n\ge 1$ is part
of the requested statement but is not used by the proof.
\end{remark}

\section{A finite Garden-of-Eden theory}

Throughout, $F\colon\alpha\to\alpha$ is a self-map.

\begin{definition}[\texttt{IsGardenOfEden}]\label{def:goe}
A state $y\in\alpha$ is a \emph{Garden of Eden} for $F$ if it has no preimage:
\[
  \GoE(F,y) :\iff \forall x,\ F(x)\ne y.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{exists\_garden\_of\_eden\_iff\_not\_surjective}]\label{thm:goeiff}
\[
  \bigl(\exists\,y,\ \GoE(F,y)\bigr) \iff \neg\,\Surj(F).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
``$\exists y\,\forall x,\ F(x)\ne y$'' is the literal negation of surjectivity
``$\forall y\,\exists x,\ F(x)=y$''; the equivalence is a propositional
simplification. It identifies the existence of unreachable states with loss of
surjectivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{iterate\_descends}]\label{lem:desc}
Let $(P,\le)$ be a partial order and $F\colon P\to P$ satisfy $F(x)\le x$ for all
$x$. Then $F^{[n+1]}(x)\le F^{[n]}(x)$ for all $n,x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
Write $F^{[n+1]}(x)=F\bigl(F^{[n]}(x)\bigr)$ and apply the descent hypothesis at
$F^{[n]}(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{finite\_garden\_of\_eden\_descent}]\label{thm:descent}
Let $(P,\le)$ be a finite partial order with $N:=|P|$ and let $F\colon P\to P$ be
monotone with $F(x)\le x$ for all $x$. Then for every $x\in P$ there exists
$n\le N$ with $F^{[n]}(x)=F^{[n+1]}(x)$; i.e.\ every orbit reaches a fixed point
within $N$ steps.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
Suppose for contradiction $F^{[n]}(x)\ne F^{[n+1]}(x)$ for all $n\le N$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:desc} the orbit is weakly descending; with no consecutive equality
it is strictly descending on $\{0,\dots,N\}$, so $k\mapsto F^{[k]}(x)$ is
injective there. This yields $N+1$ distinct elements of $P$, contradicting
$|P|=N$. Hence some consecutive pair coincides at index $n\le N$, a fixed point.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
$N=|P|$ is a hard, structure-derived deadline: change in a finite descending
system cannot persist longer than the number of states.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{finite\_garden\_of\_eden\_of\_not\_surjective}]\label{thm:goeimg}
A non-surjective monotone descending map on a finite partial order has a Garden of
Eden lying outside its eventual image---a permanently unreachable state.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:goeiff} non-surjectivity yields a Garden-of-Eden state. The
images $F^{[n]}(P)$ form a descending chain of finite sets and stabilize; the
absence of a one-step preimage places a witnessing state outside the eventual
image, so it is produced by no positive number of iterations.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{finite\_configuration\_garden\_of\_eden}]\label{thm:config}
On a finite configuration space, every non-surjective map possesses a
configuration that is never reachable as an output.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
A specialization of Theorems~\ref{thm:goeiff}--\ref{thm:goeimg} to finite
configuration spaces, where a Garden-of-Eden configuration directly witnesses
non-surjectivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{preinjective\_of\_surjective\_on\_finite\_configurations}]\label{thm:mm}
Finite Moore--Myhill shadow: on a finite type, surjectivity of $F$ implies
injectivity of $F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
For a self-map of a finite set, surjective $\Leftrightarrow$ injective
$\Leftrightarrow$ bijective; thus surjectivity forces injectivity. This is the
finite, elementary shadow of the Moore--Myhill Garden-of-Eden theorem, in which
surjectivity is equivalent to pre-injectivity.
\end{proof}

\section{Worked examples}

\paragraph{Fibonacci dictionary at $n=12$.} $F_{12}=144$ has Fibonacci divisors
$F_3=2$, $F_4=3$, $F_6=8$, $F_{12}=144$; the indices $\{3,4,6,12\}$ are exactly
the divisors of $12$ that are $\ge 3$, illustrating Theorem~\ref{thm:fibiff}.
Similarly $F_{15}=610=2\cdot 5\cdot 61$ is divisible by $F_3=2$ and $F_5=5$ but
not by $F_4,F_6,F_9$, since $3,5\mid 15$ while $4,6,9\nmid 15$.

\paragraph{Pigeonhole at $n=4$.} On $[1,8]$ the odd-part chains are
$1\!-\!2\!-\!4\!-\!8$, $3\!-\!6$, $5$, $7$. Any $5$ choices repeat a chain;
$\{3,4,5,7,8\}$ repeats it via $4\mid 8$. The extremal set $\{5,6,7,8\}$ has no
divisibility pair, so $n+1$ is sharp.

\paragraph{Descent at $N=8$.} With $P=\{0,\dots,7\}$ and $F(x)=\max(x-1,0)$, the
orbit from $7$ reaches the fixed point $0$ in $7\le N$ steps
(Theorem~\ref{thm:descent}); the image $\{0,\dots,6\}$ omits $7$, which is a
Garden of Eden (Theorem~\ref{thm:goeiff}).

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Fibonacci divisibility oracle.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:fibiff},
deciding $F_m\mid F_n$ for $m\ge 3$ reduces to testing $m\mid n$, replacing an
exponential-size computation by one $O(\log n)$ modular reduction.

\paragraph{B. Constructive pair extraction.} Given $S\subseteq[1,2n]$ with
$|S|=n+1$, hash each $x$ by $\oddpart(x)$; the first collision $q$ yields
$a,b$ with the same odd part, and the one with the smaller $2$-adic exponent
divides the other ($O(|S|)$ time), by Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon}.

\paragraph{C. Bounded stabilization detector.} For monotone descending $F$ on a
finite poset of size $N$, iterate from $x$ and stop at the first fixed point;
Theorem~\ref{thm:descent} guarantees termination within $N$ steps.

\section{Applications and discussion}

Theorem~\ref{thm:fibiff} turns divisibility queries among Fibonacci (and Lucas)
sequences into index arithmetic, underlying rank-of-apparition heuristics.
Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon} is the prototypical chain-in-a-divisor-poset argument:
the odd-part coloring partitions $[1,2n]$ into $n$ divisibility chains and forces
a repeat; the extremal family is $\{n+1,\dots,2n\}$. Theorems~\ref{thm:goeiff}--%
\ref{thm:mm} give a finite, explicit Garden-of-Eden theory with a tight
termination bound, relevant to greedy optimizers, relaxation labelings, and
confluent rewriting.

The three developments share one design principle: identify the invariant a map
preserves---divisibility via $\gcd(F_m,F_n)=F_{\gcd(m,n)}$; the $2$-adic
valuation via the odd part; the order via finite descent---then read the
conclusion off the invariant, avoiding brute force in favour of a structural
squeeze.

\section{Future directions}

\emph{(Reproduced from the Phase A program notes.)} The formalization in
\texttt{KernelTailBounds.lean} proves a deterministic scaling-law engine: given a
two-sided polynomial tail bound on a loss sequence, every standard downstream
consequence (positivity, matching rates, data rescaling, two-sided capacity
thresholds) follows. Two directions: (1) prove the $p$-series integral comparison
for $\lambda_k=(k+1)^{-p}$, deriving for $p>1$ that $\sum_{k\ge n}(k+1)^{-p}$ is
pinched between constant multiples of $(n+1)^{-(p-1)}$, upgrading a pointwise
spectral statement to a full tail-mass scaling law; (2) formalize certified
empirical spectra, turning finite eigenvalue estimates with a proven two-sided
confidence envelope into a verified tail-bound instance so that a measured
power-law slope becomes a machine-checked loss-exponent guarantee.

